WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
Confidence intervals.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.517
A bare number hides how soft it is.

3
00:00:04.517 --> 00:00:05.667
Intervals fix that.

4
00:00:06.867 --> 00:00:13.800
A confidence interval is a range built to capture the true value in ninety five percent of repeated experiments.

5
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.977
It upgrades a point estimate.

6
00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:18.178
Point eight four hides everything.

7
00:00:18.178 --> 00:00:23.033
An interval from point seven nine to point eight nine shows how soft it is.

8
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:26.312
The ninety five percent describes the procedure.

9
00:00:26.312 --> 00:00:29.600
One interval either contains the truth or it doesn't.

10
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.000
Picture one true value we're trying to measure.

11
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.854
Run an experiment and you get an interval around your estimate.

12
00:00:36.854 --> 00:00:39.900
Run it again, and each one lands a little differently.

13
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.933
Most of them straddle the truth.

14
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:44.673
But occasionally, by bad luck, one misses.

15
00:00:44.673 --> 00:00:46.067
That's the five percent.

16
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:48.867
Two practical facts follow.

17
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:54.333
More data gives a narrower interval, with width shrinking like one over the square root of n.

18
00:00:54.633 --> 00:00:59.700
And if two models have heavily overlapping intervals, you probably can't tell them apart yet.

19
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.019
How do you build one without formulas?

20
00:01:03.019 --> 00:01:03.800
The bootstrap.

21
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.167
Resample your data with replacement.

22
00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:08.800
Recompute the statistic, like the mean.

23
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.667
Repeat thousands of times.

24
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:14.800
Take the two point five and ninety seven point five percentiles.

25
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.367
Let's do it in NumPy.

26
00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:20.067
We start with sixty measured planet radii.

27
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:24.933
Then resample them ten thousand times, with replacement, and take each mean.

28
00:01:25.233 --> 00:01:28.467
The middle ninety five percent of those means is the interval.

29
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:31.667
Here's how it sounds in a real review.

30
00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:34.833
Leadership asks whether the new RAG pipeline is better.

31
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:42.567
You bootstrap per question scores: version two at point eight four, version one at point eight one, intervals overlapping.

32
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:46.294
So you say you need about two hundred more eval questions.

33
00:01:46.294 --> 00:01:48.067
That's measurement, not vibes.

34
00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:50.015
Let's recap.

35
00:01:50.015 --> 00:01:54.567
A confidence interval captures the truth ninety five percent of the time.

36
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:56.200
More data narrows it.

37
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.733
The bootstrap builds one without formulas.

38
00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:03.367
And a good report gives the interval, the overlap, and a sample size plan.
